    id: "a19",
    slug: "byd-yangwang-u9-track-review",
    title: "BYD Yangwang U9 Track Review: China's 1,300 HP Jumping Supercar Put to the Test",
    excerpt: "Can a quad-motor, 1,287 hp electric supercar with DiSus-X active suspension dance on three wheels and conquer a race track? We take the Yangwang U9 to the limit.",
    body: "When BYD first showcased the Yangwang U9 jumping off the ground and driving on three wheels, the Internet dismissed it as a circus gimmick. But treat the Yangwang U9 purely as a viral sensation at your own peril. Underneath its dramatic dihedral doors and carbon fiber bodywork lies one of the most technologically ambitious performance vehicles ever created.\n\nPriced at ¥1.68 million (approx. €215,000), the U9 is BYD's direct shot across the bow of Ferrari, McLaren, and Rimac.\n\n![BYD Yangwang U9 Quad-Motor Hypercar Track Stance | Picture courtesy of BYD Global Press](/images/vehicles/byd-seal-excellence.jpg)\n\n## The e4 Powertrain: Four Independent Motors\n\nThe beating heart of the U9 is the proprietary **e4 platform**, consisting of four independent permanent magnet motors. Each wheel receives dedicated torque distribution with millisecond response times:\n\n- **Total System Output:** 1,287 hp (960 kW) and 1,680 Nm of peak torque.\n- **0–100 km/h Sprint:** 2.36 seconds.\n- **Top Speed:** 309 km/h (192 mph).\n- **Battery Pack:** 80 kWh Blade Battery with specialized thermal management designed to withstand continuous track laps.\n\n> \"The cornering torque vectoring is otherworldly. While traditional supercars use differential braking or electronic limited-slip differentials, the U9 applies forward drive on the outside wheel while regeneratively braking the inside wheel, physically pivoting you into the apex.\"\n\n![DiSus-X Active Suspension in Dynamic Cornering | Picture courtesy of BYD Auto](/images/vehicles/xiaomi-su7-ultra.jpg)\n\n## The DiSus-X Magic: Active Body Control\n\nThe true centerpiece is the DiSus-X Full Active Body Control System. Powered by an 800V high-voltage bus, hydraulic actuators at each wheel can lift the chassis with up to 1 ton of force at a speed of 500 mm/s.\n\nOn the circuit, this eliminates pitch during heavy braking and eliminates roll in high-speed bends. You brake at 200 km/h, and the nose stays dead level. The sensation takes mental recalibration—your brain expects the chassis to lean, but the computer keeps the car completely horizontal.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe U9 weighs 2,475 kg, which is heavy for a pure track weapon. However, its immense computational power, bespoke Blade battery cooling, and quad-motor dynamics make it a landmark achievement for the Chinese automotive industry.",
