    id: "a23",
    slug: "ferrari-12cilindri-pure-v12",
    title: "Ferrari 12Cilindri: The Glorious 830 HP Naturally Aspirated V12 GT",
    excerpt: "In an era of downsizing and electrification, Ferrari gives us an 830 hp, 9,500 rpm front-mid naturally aspirated masterpiece inspired by the 365 GTB/4 Daytona.",
    body: "Maranello could have easily added twin turbochargers or a trio of electric motors to its flagship front-engine berlinetta. Instead, Ferrari chose pure, unadulterated operatic heritage. The Ferrari 12Cilindri is a celebration of the naturally aspirated V12 engine that has defined the brand since Enzo built the 125 S in 1947.\n\nDrawing unmistakable visual inspiration from Leonardo Fioravanti's iconic 1968 Ferrari 365 GTB/4 Daytona, the 12Cilindri combines retro-futuristic muscularity with state-of-the-art aerodynamics.\n\n![Ferrari 12Cilindri Front-Engine Berlinetta | Picture courtesy of Ferrari Media Center](/images/vehicles/ferrari-296-gtb.jpg)\n\n## The F140HD V12: 9,500 RPM of Analog Euphoria\n\nDisplacing 6.5 liters, the 65° V12 engine (code F140HD) benefits from Formula 1-derived titanium connecting rods and a lighter crankshaft:\n\n- **Maximum Output:** 830 HP at 9,250 rpm.\n- **Maximum Rev Limit:** 9,500 RPM.\n- **0–100 km/h:** 2.9 seconds.\n- **0–200 km/h:** 7.9 seconds.\n- **Top Speed:** Over 340 km/h (211 mph).\n\n> \"Pulling past 8,000 rpm in third gear is a transcendent experience. The exhaust note rises from a deep metallic brass into a spine-chilling acoustic crescendo that no turbocharged or electric powertrain can ever replicate.\"\n\n![Active Aerodynamic Flaps and Sculpted Rear Diffuser | Picture courtesy of Ferrari S.p.A.](/images/vehicles/ferrari-sf90-xx-stradale.jpg)\n\n## Chassis Dynamics: Independent Four-Wheel Steering\n\nThe 12Cilindri is equipped with Ferrari's **Virtual Short Wheelbase 3.0** and independent rear-wheel steering, which allows each rear wheel to adjust toe angle independently for ultra-fast lane changes and razor-sharp turn-in.\n\nStarting at €395,000 for the Coupe and €435,000 for the Spider, the 12Cilindri is not merely a grand tourer—it is the definitive, triumphant last stand of the pure naturally aspirated internal combustion engine.",
    category: "Review",
    segments: ["Sports Cars", "Luxury"],
    author: authors[0],
    publishedAt: "2026-06-09",
    readTime: "7 min read",
    featured: true,
    editorsPick: false,
    heroArticle: false,
