    excerpt: "The aggressive dual-stripe fastback that defined American motorsport dominance and captured Hollywood legend.",
    body: "In 1967, Carroll Shelby took the world's favorite pony car and transformed it into an uncompromising, asphalt-shredding monster. While the earlier GT350 was a stripped-out, high-strung track racer, the GT500 introduced effortless big-block torque to the street.\n\nUnder the elongated fiberglass hood sat a modified 428-cubic-inch (7.0-liter) Police Interceptor V8 crowned with dual Holley four-barrel carburetors, producing a conservative factory rating of **355 horsepower and 420 lb-ft of torque**.\n\n![1967 Shelby GT500 Fastback in Wimbledon White with Guardsman Blue Stripes | Picture courtesy of Shelby Archives](/images/vehicles/1967-shelby-gt500.jpg)\n\n## The Design: Functional Aggression\n\nShelby stylists replaced standard Mustang sheet metal with lightweight fiberglass components:\n\n- Twin functional hood scoops with locking pins.\n- Upper and lower quarter-panel brake cooling scoops.\n- Extended ducktail rear spoiler integrated into the decklid.\n- Horizontal sequential tail lamps sourced from the 1967 Mercury Cougar.\n\n> \"The 1967 GT500 was the first car I built that was truly comfortable to drive cross-country, but could still smoke the rear tires for an entire city block.\"\n\nOn the road, the deep V8 rumble through dual exhausts is unmistakable. Mated to a Toploader 4-speed manual gearbox and heavy-duty 9-inch rear axle, 0-60 mph took just 6.2 seconds—blisteringly quick for 1967.\n\nImmortalized in automotive folklore as 'Eleanor' in *Gone in 60 Seconds*, the 1967 Shelby GT500 remains the quintessential American muscle car dream.",
