    body: "When the Jaguar E-Type (XK-E in North America) debuted at the Geneva Motor Show in March 1961, it caused an immediate international sensation. It wasn't just fast; it was breathtakingly gorgeous. Even Enzo Ferrari famously declared it 'the most beautiful car ever made.'\n\nPriced at roughly half the cost of a comparable Ferrari or Aston Martin, the E-Type democratized 150 mph performance for British and global sports car enthusiasts.\n\n![1961 Jaguar E-Type Series 1 3.8 Fixed Head Coupe | Picture courtesy of British Motor Heritage](/images/vehicles/1961-jaguar-e-type-coupe.jpg)\n\n## Aircraft Engineering on Wheels\n\nDesigned by aerodynamicist Malcolm Sayer, the E-Type utilized a revolutionary monocoque aircraft construction with a front tubular subframe supporting the engine and suspension:\n\n- **Engine:** 3.8-liter DOHC straight-six with triple SU carburetors producing **265 HP and 260 lb-ft of torque**.\n- **Suspension:** Independent rear suspension with inboard disc brakes, designed by Bob Knight.\n- **Top Speed:** 150 mph (241 km/h).\n- **Interior:** Glass-covered headlights, toggle switches, Smiths gauges, and aluminum dashboard trim.\n\n> \"The E-Type didn't just compete with Italian exotics—it out-engineered them while costing less than half the price.\"\n\nToday, the Series 1 E-Type is recognized by modern art museums worldwide as a high watermark of industrial design.",
