    slug: "1971-lamborghini-miura-sv",
    title: "1971 Lamborghini Miura SV: The Birth of the Mid-Engine Supercar",
    excerpt: "Marcello Gandini's breathtaking silhouette paired with split-sump lubrication and a screaming transverse V12.",
    body: "Before the Lamborghini Miura, high-performance grand tourers placed their engines in front of the driver. Young Lamborghini engineers Gian Paolo Dallara, Paolo Stanzani, and test driver Bob Wallace worked after hours to design a transverse mid-engine chassis inspired by the Ford GT40.\n\nWrapped in Marcello Gandini's timeless Bertone bodywork, the Miura debuted at the 1966 Geneva Salon and overnight rendered front-engine Ferraris obsolete.\n\n![1971 Lamborghini Miura SV (Spinto Veloce) | Picture courtesy of Automobili Lamborghini](/images/vehicles/1971-lamborghini-miura-sv.jpg)\n\n## The Ultimate Miura: The SV\n\nThe 1971 Miura SV (Spinto Veloce) fixed the earlier cars' high-speed aerodynamic lift and oiling quirks:\n\n- **Split Sump:** Separated engine and gearbox lubrication to prevent metal shavings from entering the engine.\n- **Power:** 3.9-liter 60° V12 producing **385 HP at 7,850 rpm**.\n- **Widened Rear Fenders:** Accommodated 9-inch wide rear Campagnolo wheels.\n- **Headlight Eyelashes Removed:** Giving the front end a clean, purposeful look.\n\nThe Miura SV was the car that established Lamborghini as a permanent titan of exotic performance.",
