    excerpt: "Undefeated in 29 consecutive Japanese Touring Car races, introducing ATTESA E-TS AWD and HICAS four-wheel steering.",
    body: "When the Nissan Skyline GT-R R32 debuted in 1989 to compete in Group A touring car racing, it didn't just win—it annihilated the competition. In the Japanese Touring Car Championship (JTCC), the R32 won all 29 races it entered from 1989 to 1993.\n\nWhen it entered the Australian Touring Car Championship and crushed the local Ford Sierra Cosworths at the Bathurst 1000, Australian motoring press famously dubbed it 'Godzilla'—the monster from Japan.\n\n![1989 Nissan Skyline GT-R R32 in Gun Grey Metallic | Picture courtesy of Nissan Heritage](/images/vehicles/1989-nissan-skyline-gtr-r32.jpg)\n\n## Tech Ahead of Its Time\n\n- **RB26DETT Engine:** 2.6-liter twin-turbo inline-six with cast iron block, officially rated at the Japanese 'gentlemen's agreement' limit of **276 HP** (real output was 320+ hp in street trim, easily tunable to 600+ hp).\n- **ATTESA E-TS:** Advanced electro-hydraulic all-wheel drive that ran 100% rear-wheel drive until rear slip was detected, vectoring up to 50% torque to the front wheels.\n- **Super HICAS:** Electronically controlled four-wheel steering system.\n\nThe R32 GT-R pioneered the digital performance era and established the foundation for modern supercar AWD systems.",
