    title: "1968 Dodge Charger R/T 440: The Quintessential American Villain Car",
    excerpt: "Richard Sias' coke-bottle silhouette, hidden headlights, and 440 cubic inches of pure American asphalt-tearing menace.",
    body: "When the redesigned 1968 Dodge Charger hit the streets, it revolutionized muscle car styling. Moving away from the fastback roofline of the 1966-67 generation, stylist Richard Sias introduced the famous 'coke-bottle' profile with sculpted scallops in the doors and hood, flying buttress rear pillars, and vacuum-operated hidden headlights.\n\nUnder the hood of the R/T (Road/Track) model sat the mighty **440-cubic-inch (7.2-liter) Magnum V8**, churning out 375 horsepower and a massive 480 lb-ft of torque.\n\n![1968 Dodge Charger R/T in Triple Black | Picture courtesy of Mopar Media](/images/vehicles/1968-dodge-charger-rt-440.jpg)\n\n## Cinematic Immortality\n\nIn the 1968 film *Bullitt*, a black 1968 Charger 440 driven by stuntman Bill Hickman engaged in the most famous car chase in cinema history against Steve McQueen's Highland Green Mustang through the hilly streets of San Francisco.\n\nWith heavy-duty torsion bars, extra-leaf rear springs, and front anti-roll bars, the Charger R/T delivered genuine highway capability along with thunderous straight-line acceleration.",
